# Line endings (#125).
#
# Without this file, the working-tree line endings of every text file depend on
# each developer's `core.autocrlf`. On Windows that defaults to converting to
# CRLF, which breaks things that are invisible on Linux and in CI:
#
#   - anything that parses a file line-wise and compares exactly (the compose
#     healthcheck contract test read "  gateway:\r" and reported the service as
#     missing — #120/#123)
#   - shell scripts and Dockerfiles, which fail inside a Linux container with a
#     bare `bad interpreter` when they carry CRLF
#   - the shell one-liners embedded in docker-compose healthchecks
#
# CI runs on Linux only, so none of it is caught before it reaches a Windows
# developer.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect text vs binary; `eol=lf` pins the working-tree
# ending for everything it decides is text. No binary files are tracked today —
# the entries below are declared anyway so adding one later cannot be corrupted
# by normalization.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

# The scale fixtures are GENERATED with CRLF on purpose: fixtures/scale/gen_fixtures.py
# writes them through Python's csv module, whose default line terminator is CRLF
# (RFC 4180). Marking them `-text` opts them out of normalization entirely, so the
# committed bytes stay exactly what the generator produces.
#
# The alternative — normalizing them to LF — would rewrite all 12 files and then
# lose to the generator the next time someone regenerates them. Their line endings
# are already identical on every platform because they are excluded here, which is
# the property this file exists to guarantee.
# NB: `fixtures/scale/*.csv` would NOT match — a gitattributes `*` does not cross a
# directory separator, and these live one level deeper (fixtures/scale/<n>/…).
fixtures/scale/**/*.csv -text
